The quickest way to get a feel for pancake is to watch one program call another.
So let us write the calling half, look at every line, and then point you at the
[surface]($link.page('surfaces')) that matches your language. The build compiles and runs it under `zig build examples`, which is what keeps it from
drifting away from the library.

## In Zig

pancake is a [Zig](https://ziglang.org/) package, so the first step is to fetch it
into your build:

```sh
zig fetch --save git+https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake
```

```zig
const pancake = b.dependency("pancake", .{ .target = target, .optimize = optimize });
exe.root_module.addImport("pancake", pancake.module("pancake"));
```

The client itself is one object and a handful of verbs. Here is the entire
round-trip:

```zig
const pancake = @import("pancake");

// Dial by locator, the one dialer. The transport set is a bare slice naming
// the transports this client can reach (here, just TCP); `tcp.at` turns a
// `host:port` into a `tcp` locator with no caller buffer. Add `iroh` by
// putting one more netlayer in the slice, the same call.
var tcp_resident = pancake.net.tcp.Resident{ .io = io };

const conn = try pancake.driver.connect(allocator, &.{tcp_resident.netlayer()}, pancake.net.tcp.at(designator), .ephemeral, .{});
defer conn.close();

const greeter = try conn.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
var arena = std.heap.ArenaAllocator.init(allocator);
defer arena.deinit();
const settled = try conn.call(greeter, .{
    .verb = "greet",
    .args = &.{.{ .string = "world" }},
}, arena.allocator());
switch (settled) {
    .fulfilled => |arg| std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{arg.string}),
    .broken => |reason| std.debug.print("the greeter refused: {s}\n", .{reason}),
}
```

Read it as four moves:

1. **Connect.** `pancake.driver.connect` opens a session over a netlayer and hands
   you one owning `Connection`. The `defer conn.close()` on the next line is the
   only connection cleanup the program needs; the arena's `deinit` reclaims the
   reply values.
2. **Fetch a capability.** `conn.fetch("greeter")` asks the partner for the object it
   published under the name `greeter`. What comes back is a capability, not a copy, so
   from here on you are calling the partner's object, not your own.
3. **Call it.** `conn.call` sends the `greet` method with one string argument. The
   arena you pass in owns whatever the reply allocates, so you free the result by
   dropping the arena rather than tracking each piece.
4. **Switch on the outcome.** A call settles one of two ways. It is `fulfilled` with
   a value, or it is `broken` with a reason, and the compiler makes you handle both.

That snippet is checked verbatim by
[`examples/zig/readme_quick_start.zig`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/readme_quick_start.zig).
The hosting half, the program that publishes `greeter` and answers the call, has the
same shape, and
[`examples/zig/01_connect_and_serve.zig`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/01_connect_and_serve.zig)
runs both halves together. From there the
[example chapters](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/README.md)
keep going down, from this application surface all the way to the raw engine, one
`step` at a time.

## From C, or any FFI host

The same library is also a plain C ABI, and that is the door for everyone not
writing Zig. If you are embedding pancake in a C program or binding it from another
runtime, start with
[consuming pancake from C](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/consuming-from-c.md).
It walks the build, the artifacts, the ownership rules, and a first complete program
in one place. The C surface is opaque handles, fixed-width return codes, `ptr + len` views, and explicit release
functions, the shape every binding wraps.

## In Rust, TypeScript, Python, or Racket

If you write one of these four, a binding already did the wrapping for you. Each one
consumes the C ABI as a genuine foreign-language consumer and then presents an
idiomatic API on top, so you write the language you already know instead of foreign
function calls. Start at the [bindings overview]($link.page('bindings')) for the
shape they share, then jump to your language:
[Rust]($link.page('bindings/rust')),
[TypeScript]($link.page('bindings/ts')),
[Python]($link.page('bindings/python')), or
[Racket]($link.page('bindings/racket')).

## Then go deeper

Once a round-trip works, two paths open up. Read [the modules]($link.page('modules'))
to see how the library is layered and where each responsibility lives. Or read
[the concepts]($link.page('concepts')) first if capabilities, CapTP, and OCapN are
new to you and you want the ideas before the API.
